  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "You’re eager to see your new room addition take shape, and drywall feels like a huge step forward! But knowing *when* to start the drywall process is crucial for a smooth project. The key thing to remember is that drywall typically comes after several other major steps are completed and, critically, inspected. Think of it as a logical flow: first, your framing needs to be solid and passed any structural inspections. Then, the roof and exterior enclosure should be in place, keeping the elements out. After that, all your 'rough-ins'—meaning the initial electrical, plumbing, and HVAC systems—need to be installed and inspected. This is super important because once drywall goes up, all that critical infrastructure is hidden inside the walls. You can't easily fix something or get an inspection after the fact without tearing things out. Once those rough-ins are approved, insulation usually goes in, and *then* it’s time for the drywall. Don't rush it! Installing drywall prematurely can lead to big problems, like missing inspections, covering up faulty wiring, or trapping moisture. If you’re working with a professional, they’ll guide you through this timeline, ensuring every step is completed correctly before moving on to the next. That way, you'll get a beautiful, compliant finish."
    }
  ],
